https://odnca.org/docs/ODNCA-STD-011-WEB3-ACTIONS-1.pdf

Scheme syntax:
ord://<name.tld>[/<path>] (name form — mutable reference)
ord://<txid>[/<vout>] (txid form — immutable; vout defaults to 0)
The authority is a naming-system identifier: with exactly one dot it is
an SNS name per ODNCA-STD-003; without a dot it is an OpNS name. UTF-8
throughout, no whitespace or control characters. <path> is an optional
slash-prefixed route within the named site; an absent path is
equivalent to "/". Resolution is performed against on-chain state; no
DNS interaction occurs at any point.

Scheme semantics: per ODNCA-STD-005 §4 — the name form loads the
content currently coupled to the name (the coupling follows the name);
the txid form always loads that exact inscription.

Encoding considerations: the authority component carries UTF-8 names;
percent-encoding per RFC 3986 applies. No IDNA/punycode processing —
names are not DNS labels.

Interoperability considerations: the scheme deliberately does not
resolve via DNS; applications unaware of it treat it as an unknown
scheme per RFC 3986. No default port.

Security considerations: resolution depends on the client's view of
on-chain state; clients SHOULD verify name-holder bindings against a
committed state (ODNCA commit chain) rather than trust a single index.
Dereferencing "ord" URIs never initiates payments or signing
operations. Visually confusable Unicode names carry the usual homograph
risks; client display policies apply.